  content: |
    Thanks for the heads up. I found the root cause and made changes so delete no longer triggers JSON parsing on an empty body.

    What I changed
    - Backend delete returns JSON instead of 204
      - `DELETE /api/users/{id}` now responds `200` with `{"ok": true}` to avoid any client calling `response.json()` on an empty body.
      - File: backend/ddms/api/routes_users.py:1
    - Frontend API client already handles 204/empty bodies gracefully
      - File: frontend/src/lib/api.ts:1
    - Users list fetch uses trailing slash to avoid redirects
      - File: frontend/src/app/pages/Users.tsx:1

    What to do now
    - Hard refresh the browser (or open an incognito window).
    - On Users page, delete a non-owner user again. The “Unexpected end of JSON input” error should be gone.

    If you still see it, tell me the exact toast text and check the browser Network tab for the delete request:
    - Status code and response headers/body for `DELETE /api/users/{id}`
    - Any 307/redirects or CORS warnings

    Once you confirm it’s resolved, I can commit this fix with a clear, single-sentence title describing the user-visible impact.
